        "index": 1,
        "start": 0,
        "end": 19.92,
        "text": "An investigation begins with a decision, not a verdict. In this synthetic case, we want to distinguish one operator from shared infrastructure. Pivotglass keeps the question, the evidence, and alternative explanations together. Novice coaching starts simply: What data do you have? What happened?"
      },
      {
        "index": 2,
        "start": 19.92,
        "end": 42,
        "text": "Do you know how it started? Here, we do not. Keep that unknown. The coach proposes questions, and the analyst edits one to ask what independent evidence would distinguish common control from shared hosting. Review before saving. Practice records participation and reflection; expertise grows through real work and feedback."
      },
      {
        "index": 3,
        "start": 42,
        "end": 61.2,
        "text": "Now preview a local report. Candidate strings are separate from admitted evidence. Select the domain and long URL, then explicitly admit them with their source. The library retains the source digest. A successful preview is not admission, and a document claim is not automatically true."
      },
      {
        "index": 4,
        "start": 61.2,
        "end": 80.8,
        "text": "Open the indicator. Read the complete value, typed relationships, original source, and collection history. The file communicates with a domain, but topology alone does not establish ownership. Keep both hypotheses. Record the conflicting explanation and what independent collection could resolve it."
      },
      {
        "index": 5,
        "start": 80.8,
        "end": 103.52,
        "text": "Link evidence to a hypothesis with an explicit stance and rationale. The competing-hypotheses matrix lets us compare the same evidence across alternatives. Then record a structured technique run: inputs, analysis, limitations, and disposition. Required fields make the work inspectable; the analyst still evaluates the argument."
      },
      {
        "index": 6,
        "start": 103.52,
        "end": 124.8,
        "text": "Coverage shows where the investigation has evidence and where it needs more. It does not measure danger. Likelihood asks how probable an explanation is. Confidence asks how sound and sufficient its evidence and reasoning are. Keep the collection requirement and stopping condition visible so the hunt has a purpose and a boundary."
      },
      {
        "index": 7,
        "start": 124.8,
        "end": 149.76,
        "text": "Generate the workspace report. Review questions, alternatives, explicit evidence links, technique results, unresolved contradictions, and next steps. Save Markdown for an editable handoff, or print to PDF. Before sharing, check source handling and limitations. The next analyst should be able to reconstruct the case, challenge the judgment, and continue the work."
      }
